#!/usr/bin/python3
# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
#
# bc5-gpu-stall-sampler: BC5 (exp 0373, stage T0 of exp 0370's design D1-D8). A resident, non-halting root sampler
# for the F403 GPU hang class on the BC-250 (gfx1013, Cyan Skillfish). Python 3, stdlib only (the box host's 3.14).
#
# What it does:
#  * Every 25 ms it reads debugfs amdgpu_fence_info and runs a per-ring "stall clock": the time since that ring's
#    "Last emitted" or "Last signaled fence" last changed. drm_sched re-arms the kernel's ring timeout at every push and
#    every completion (drm_sched_start_timeout from drm_sched_job_begin and drm_sched_get_finished_job, Linux v7.2
#    8d3ae59288f1 drivers/gpu/drm/scheduler/sched_main.c:198-302,923-948), so this clock is the kernel's within one
#    poll (F496; lockup_timeout 2000 ms, exp 0259).
#  * A ring is stalled when emitted != signaled and nothing changed for 0.5 s. At stall clock 0.5, 0.8, 1.1, 1.4 and
#    1.7 s it takes one sample: the CP/GRBM registers through amdgpu_regs and the fences. Only with --wave-scan does
#    a sample also scan every wave slot through amdgpu_wave (STATUS, PC, EXEC, HW_ID1/2 and the rest of the kernel's
#    record): off by default since exp 0374, whose T1 lost the machine to a GPU hang 106 ms after a wave scan under
#    load (suspected, not proven). It never reads GPRs, never halts a wave, never runs umr and never forks. The
#    samples stay in RAM.
#  * It writes an event directory when /dev/kmsg shows amdgpu's "ring <name> timeout" line, or when the stall clock
#    passes 1.9 s, whichever comes first, then fsyncs it and chowns it to the owner (files 600, directories 700).
#    A stall that resolves earlier adds one census line to stalls.log.
#  * It writes nothing to /dev/kmsg (F420) and needs no process start at the event (F112).
#  * Since exp 0377 (F529) every register sample also reads the compute dispatch registers (COMPUTE_PGM_LO/HI, DIM,
#    START, NUM_THREAD, PGM_RSRC1-3, DISPATCH_INITIATOR, DISPATCH_ID) of the gfx ME, unbanked (see COMPUTE_BANK), and
#    decodes the program address (PGM_HI:PGM_LO << 8) into regs-N.txt, summary.txt and the census line.
#
# Usage, as root:
#   bc5-gpu-stall-sampler --owner USER --out DIR     the service (bc5-gpu-stall-sampler.service, from install.sh)
#   bc5-gpu-stall-sampler --find                     print the amdgpu debugfs directory it would use, then exit
#   bc5-gpu-stall-sampler --once [--idle-ok] [--wave-scan]
#                                                    one sample now, printed (registers in umr's "=>" format; the
#                                                    waves only with --wave-scan; --regs-only is the default)
#   --wave-scan (service or --once): allow wave scans at all; without it nothing reads amdgpu_wave (exp 0374)
# Usage, any user, no device:
#   bc5-gpu-stall-sampler --test                     built-in self-test: synthetic fence traces and the wave decoder
#   bc5-gpu-stall-sampler --replay TRACE [--out DIR] replay a fence/kmsg trace (format: see read_trace)
#   bc5-gpu-stall-sampler --list-regs                the registers: name, byte offset, sampled or skipped
#   bc5-gpu-stall-sampler --compare-check FILE       read install.sh's sampler-versus-umr check file, print a table
# Forced samples for T1/T2, as the owner, no root: write a file named "force" into DIR, e.g. "echo once > DIR/force".
#   Tokens: once (default; the file is removed after the sample) | N (one sample every N >= 5 s while the file exists)
#   | regs (registers only, the default) | waves (also scan the wave slots: only when the service runs with
#   --wave-scan, otherwise refused in the census line) | idle (also when no job is pending). Results go to
#   DIR/forced-<date>-<time>/. Writing a new request replaces one still waiting for a job (exp 0374).
#
# Output in DIR (default ~OWNER/bc5-data/captures/gpu-stall): stalls.log (census, one line per stall >= 0.5 s, plus
# start and forced lines), event-<date>-<time>-<ring>/ (summary.txt, fence.txt, kmsg.txt, regs-N.txt, waves-N.txt,
# waves-N.bin), forced-<date>-<time>/. waves-N.bin: b"BC5WAVE1", u32 record count, u32 dwords per record (17), then
# per record u8 se, sa, wgp, simd, wave, 3 pad bytes, and the 17 raw little-endian dwords that amdgpu_wave returned.
#
# Sources (rule 4; read 2026-10-29): Linux v7.2 = github.com/torvalds/linux commit 8d3ae59288f1e7d58d76558a6ee96d533bc5019f
# (tag v7.2, 2026-08-16); umr = gitlab.freedesktop.org/tomstdenis/umr commit aa7f72dcb8bd33af27c6a0b6a3e173644dc3a800.
# The box runs 7.2.9-ogc3.1: TODO(verify) every v7.2 citation below against that tree at T1.

